e alignment is by timestamp. The spec suggests aligning by meaning. But we could approximate that the series is aligned.\n\nWe need to decide: perhaps we can compute delta between start times for each anchor. Since they are probably aligned, we might find small differences (within +-0.5 seconds). We can then compute median difference and categorize.\n\nThus we may approximate that the subtitles are aligned: maybe a constant offset of a few hundred milliseconds.\n\nIf we have the content, we can compute exact timings.\n\nThus we can fulfill step 6 alignment.\n\nAfter alignment, to translate each phrase, we can use the English subtitle cues (if aligned) as translation source. If misaligned, we will do machine translation; but the spec says to use translation capability for unmatched phrases.\n\nThus we attempt to pair each phrase with an English cue (subtitle line) if aligned.\n\nThus next step: phrase selection. We'll need to produce usage notes and quiz.\n\n**Flashcards optional:** Not requested yet. But user didn't ask for flashcards. So no flashcards.\n\n**Check for earlier sheet:** none.\n\n**Prompt to save:** will ask user for confirmation after building sheet but before saving.\n\n**Tag video:** will ask after save.\n\nThus after reading subtitles and performing selection, we need to construct the sheet text. Then confirm.\n\nGiven the complexity, we need to be careful to follow the required instruction format for saving. After generating sheet text and file name, we need to display a preview (summary) to user (alignment line, earlier sheet, phrase list with time and phrase, translation) – but not paste whole sheet. Then ask: \"Save the study sheet here? Reply yes, yes with flashcards, or no.\"\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n- Output to user a summary message including maybe alignment status and sheet location.\n\nThus the conversation flow:\n\n1. Summarize input (yet we already have to summarize after step 1). Actually we have processed step 2 already. We must no